[Event "Benoni: White's setup, and the gambit refuted"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/3Ll0yprL/yHaXNbKj"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Welcome to the Benoni with White, built around a wedge: a pawn on d5 that cramps every black piece behind it and is brutally hard to shift. Start with the queen pawn. [%cal Yd2d4] }
1. d4 { Pawn to d4: the move this whole repertoire is built on. Without it on the board, the Benoni you are about to meet cannot exist. } (1. e4 { Pushing the king pawn to e4 is fine chess, but it is a different opening entirely: the Benoni only ever arises after the queen pawn. } 1... c5 { Meeting e4 with c5 walks straight into the Sicilian Defence, a completely different opening with its own book. Today we stick to the queen pawn instead. }) 1... Nf6 { He develops the knight to f6 first. Follow with the c pawn to c4: two pawns side by side in the centre is the invitation that draws him into a Benoni. [%cal Yc2c4] } 2. c4 { Pawns on c4 and d4 now share the centre between them, exactly the shape that tempts him to strike at d4 with his c pawn and start a Benoni. } (2. Nf3 { Developing the knight to f3 first is sound chess, just a different order. This repertoire always plays the c pawn first, so every game reaches the same shape. } 2... c5 3. d5 e6 { This still reaches a Benoni, but without a pawn already on c4 he has extra tries along the way. Playing the c pawn first avoids all of them. }) (2. Bf4 { Bishop to f4 is the London System, a completely different opening with its own study in this collection, not the Benoni fight we are building here. } 2... c5 3. e3 { Playing the pawn to e3 here is solid London chess, but it never lets the centre pawns clash the way they do in the Benoni we are learning today. }) 2... c5 { This is the Benoni: he strikes at d4 from the side with his c pawn. Do not capture and do not defend it. Push straight past to d5, planting the wedge. [%cal Yd4d5] } 3. d5 { The pawn lands on d5, the wedge. It cramps every black piece that wants to cross the centre, and every plan in this opening grows out of defending it. [%csl Gd5] } (3. dxc5 { Capturing on c5 hands the pawn straight back, and with it every scrap of the space advantage the queen pawn push was supposed to win. } 3... e6 4. Nf3 Bxc5 { His bishop recaptures on c5 and the position is dead level, no wedge, no cramp, nothing to show for it. Push the pawn past to d5 instead and keep the wedge. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (3. Nf3 { Developing the knight to f3 is a perfectly fine move, but it invites him to trade on d4 at once, and once that trade happens the Benoni never gets started. } 3... cxd4 4. Nxd4 { The knight recaptures on d4 and the position opens up level, a normal game with no wedge at all. Pushing past to d5 instead is what this whole repertoire is built on. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (3. e3 { Pawn to e3 is too modest here: it stops one square short of d5, so there is never a wedge to plant and never any cramp on his pieces. } 3... cxd4 4. exd4 d5 { After the trade on d4 he plants his own pawn on d5, matching you in the centre with no cramp on his side at all. Pushing past on move three avoids this entirely. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (3. Nc3 { Developing the knight to c3 looks natural, but it leaves the pawn on d4 undefended, and he simply takes it. } 3... cxd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 { The queen has to recapture on d4 and sits out in the open far too early. His knight to c6 develops with tempo, hitting her and gaining time. [%cal Rc6d4] }) 3... e6 { He pushes the e pawn to bite at the wedge. Develop the knight to c3 first: it guards d5, so if he trades there, the c pawn is free to recapture. [%cal Yb1c3] } 4. Nc3 { The knight settles on c3 and guards d5 before he can even try to remove it. If he trades on d5 now, the c pawn recaptures, and the wedge never leaves the board. } (4. Nf3 { Developing the knight to f3 first is also good, and it usually transposes back into the same Benoni. This repertoire plays knight to c3 first because it guards d5 right away. } 4... exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 { This is the very same Benoni structure, wedge on d5, knight guarding it from c3, just reached through a different move order. }) (4. dxe6 { Capturing on e6 gives away the whole point of the opening: it opens his f column for free and, once he recaptures, lets him build a pawn centre of his own. } 4... fxe6 5. Nc3 d5 { He plants pawns on d5 and e6 side by side, his own pawn centre, while yours has vanished entirely. That trade on e6 handed him exactly this. [%csl Rd5,Re6] }) (4. e4 { Pushing to e4 here is a move too early: nothing defends the pawn yet, since the knight has not reached c3. } 4... Nxe4 { His knight simply takes the pawn on e4 for free. Develop the knight to c3 before you ever push to e4, so the pawn always has a defender waiting. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (4. g3 { Fianchettoing the bishop with g3 is a completely sound plan against the Benoni, just a different one from the setup this repertoire teaches. } 4... exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Bg2 { Fianchettoing to g2 is good chess and a real alternative. This repertoire instead pushes the pawn to e4 and develops the bishop to d3 for a more direct fight. }) (4. Bf4 { Developing the bishop to f4 immediately is playable. It just reaches the same position as the main line through a different move order. } 4... exd5 5. cxd5 d6 { This is a fine way to meet the Benoni too. This repertoire develops the knight to c3 first instead, because from there it guards the wedge on d5 right away. }) 4... exd5 { He trades on d5. Recapture with the c pawn, never the knight: a pawn on d5 is the wedge this whole opening is built around. [%cal Yc4d5] } 5. cxd5 { The pawn recaptures, so the wedge stays a pawn, not a piece. A piece can be chased or traded off in one move; a pawn sitting on d5 is far harder to shift. } (5. Nxd5 { Recapturing with the knight lets him trade it off on the very next move, handing away your best-placed piece for nothing in return. } 5... Nxd5 6. cxd5 d6 { The pawn does land on d5 and the wedge is real, but your best-developed knight is gone, and without it his position is far easier to play. }) (5. Qxd5 { Recapturing with the queen looks tempting, but look again: his knight on f6 already covers d5, so the queen would be walking onto that square for free. } 5... Nxd5 { His knight takes the queen. Trading a knight for your queen on move five is as bad as this opening ever gets. Always recapture with the c pawn instead. [%cal Rf6d5] }) 5... d6 { He plants a pawn on d6 to hold the position together. Push to e4 next: it builds a big pawn centre and adds a second permanent guard to the wedge on d5. [%cal Ye2e4] } 6. e4 { A second pawn joins the wedge on d5, guarded now by both the e pawn and the knight on c3, so there is nothing on d5 for him to win for free. } (6. Nf3 { Developing the knight to f3 before pushing the e pawn is just as good here. The pawn still reaches e4 next move, and nothing black has played can stop it. } 6... g6 7. e4 Bg7 8. h3 { This reaches the very same Benoni setup, only by a different door. This repertoire pushes the e pawn first, while nothing black has played can stop it. }) (6. f4 { Pushing to f4 here starts the Four Pawns Attack, a very sharp, double-edged weapon against the Benoni, and a whole lesson of its own. } 6... g6 7. e4 Bg7 { His fianchettoed bishop now stares straight down at your four-pawn centre. This repertoire's calmer setup never hands him a target that large to bite on. }) (6. e3 { Pawn to e3 stops one square short of where you want it. It defends nothing extra and never gains the space that a pawn on e4 would. } 6... g6 7. Nf3 Bg7 { This is fine, solid chess, but it is the pawn on e4 that guards the wedge on d5 and grabs extra space. Pawn to e3 gives you neither. [%cal Ge2e4] }) (6. Bf4 { The bishop develops to f4 and pressures d6, but his knight jumps to h5 and chases it away again immediately. } 6... Nh5 7. Be3 Nf6 { Both sides have made two moves each and the position is exactly where it started, except your pawn still has not managed to reach e4. }) 6... g6 { He fianchettoes, aiming his bishop down the long diagonal toward your queen side. Play the pawn to h3 now, before anything can pin your knight on g4. [%cal Yh2h3] } 7. h3 { This small pawn move has a big job: once the knight develops to f3, nothing black plays can ever pin it, because the g4 square is guarded. [%csl Gg4] } (7. Nf3 { Developing the knight to f3 before the pawn move is also fine. The pawn still comes to h3 next, this just reaches the same position by another order. } 7... Bg7 8. h3 { This is the identical Benoni setup either way. Playing the pawn to h3 first only shuts the g4 square one move sooner, before the bishop develops. }) (7. f4 { Pushing to f4 here is the same Four Pawns idea, just reached a move later. Sharp and fully playable, but a far more double-edged fight than this repertoire teaches. } 7... Bg7 8. Bb5+ Nfd7 { The position stays sharp with chances for both sides here. This repertoire's calmer pawn structure avoids that fight entirely. }) (7. Bd3 { The bishop is headed to the correct square, just a move too soon: without the pawn on h3 first, the knight will meet a pin the moment it develops to f3. } 7... Bg7 8. Nf3 Bg4 { His bishop pins the knight to the queen. It is not dangerous here, but playing the h pawn to h3 first rules this pin out completely, for the rest of the game. [%cal Rg4f3] }) (7. Bb5+ { The bishop checks on b5, forcing his knight to decide where it blocks. Fully playable, though it hands him a free developing move in return. } 7... Nbd7 8. Nf3 Bg7 { This is a fair game for both sides. This repertoire keeps the bishop back for d3 instead, where it also guards the pawn on e4. }) (7. Bg5 { Developing the bishop to g5 is playable, but it becomes a target the moment he plays h6, then g5, chasing it around the board. } 7... Bg7 8. Bd3 h6 9. Bf4 Nh5 { The bishop has already been chased twice, first by the pawn to h6, now by the knight jumping to h5, for a plan that gains nothing extra. }) 7... Bg7 { Now develop the knight to f3. With the pawn already on h3, nothing black owns can ever pin it there. [%cal Yg1f3] } 8. Nf3 { The knight lands on its most natural square. With the pawn already sitting on h3, no bishop of his will ever be able to pin it there. } (8. Bd3 { Developing the bishop to d3 first is also good. The knight still lands on f3 the very next move; this just changes the order. } 8... O-O 9. Nf3 { This reaches the identical setup, bishop on d3, knight on f3, pawn on h3, just with the king already tucked away on the other side. }) (8. Be3 { This is sound too, and flexible: from e3 the bishop eyes the long diagonal toward a7 without yet committing to the d3 square. } 8... O-O 9. Nf3 Re8 { This is sound chess too, just from a different setup: here the bishop develops to e3 before the knight reaches f3, with castling still sandwiched in between. }) (8. Bb5+ { This check helps him more than you: once his knight blocks, moves like a6 and b5 come with tempo, since the bishop keeps needing to move again. } 8... Nbd7 9. Nf3 a6 10. Bd3 b5 { His queen side pawns are already rolling forward, and your bishop has been kicked all the way back to d3 without gaining anything for the check. [%cal Rb5b4] }) (8. Bg5 { Developing the bishop to g5 is playable, though the pawn to h6 later forces it to decide where to go next. } 8... O-O 9. Bd3 Re8 { This is a fair game for both sides. This repertoire sends the bishop straight to d3 instead, where nothing forces it to move again. }) (8. Be2 { Developing the bishop to e2 is playable, but from there it does not guard the pawn on e4, and e4 is exactly what his coming gambit attacks. } 8... O-O 9. Nf3 b5 { Now his pawn sacrifice on b5 is genuinely dangerous, since nothing defends the pawn on e4 once his knight captures there. [%cal Rf6e4] }) 8... O-O { He tucks his king away with castling. Develop the bishop to d3: from there it guards the pawn on e4 and aims straight at his king side. [%cal Yf1d3] } 9. Bd3 { The whole setup is complete: knight, bishop and pawns all where they belong. Castle next, and keep an eye on his weak pawn on d6 for the rest of the game. [%csl Rd6] } (9. Be3 { This is a fine move too: from e3 the bishop eyes the c5 and a7 squares instead of committing itself to the d3 diagonal. } 9... Re8 10. Nd2 { Solid chess, but a different setup from this repertoire's own, which keeps the bishop on d3 in every game so it always guards e4. }) (9. Nd2 { Developing the knight to d2 is also good. From there it can jump to c4 and lean on his permanently weak pawn on d6. } 9... b6 10. Be2 Ba6 { This is playable too, a different plan entirely: he fianchettoes his own bishop toward the long diagonal instead of pushing for the gambit on b5. }) (9. Bg5 { Developing the bishop to g5 is playable, but it invites the rook to e8 and the pawn to c4, both with tempo on the bishop. } 9... Re8 10. Bd3 c4 { The pawn push to c4 gains him both space and a tempo on the bishop, exactly the kind of free move that developing to g5 invited. }) (9. Be2 { Developing the bishop to e2 is playable, but here too it fails to guard the pawn on e4, the square his gambit is really aimed at. } 9... b5 10. Bxb5 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Qa5+ 12. Nfd2 Qxb5 { His gambit pays off here: the check on a5 wins the bishop back, and the material is level again. A real fight, not the clean edge the main line keeps. [%cal Ra5b5] }) (9. Bb5 { The bishop develops to b5, but it walks straight into a fork: his queen checks on a5, hitting both your king and the undefended bishop at once. } 9... Nxe4 10. Nxe4 Qa5+ 11. Nc3 Bxc3+ 12. bxc3 Qxb5 { The queen collects the bishop, and you finish a pawn down for it, the same cost the fork was threatening from the moment the bishop landed on b5. [%cal Ra5b5,Ra5e1] }) (9. Bc4 { The bishop looks bold on c4, but it sits on the exact square his own c pawn is aiming for. Sure enough, it has to retreat, then gets chased again when that pawn arrives. } 9... Re8 10. Bd3 c4 11. Bc2 { Three moves just to land the bishop on c2, while he spent the same time putting a rook on the open e column and a pawn on c4 for free. }) 9... b5 { The Benoni gambit: he offers the b pawn to rip open lines toward your centre. Take it with the knight. The bishop stays home, still guarding e4. [%cal Yc3b5] } 10. Nxb5 { The knight captures the pawn while the bishop stays put on d3, still guarding e4. He will still go after that pawn, and you are ready for it. [%csl Ge4] } (10. Bxb5 { Capturing with the bishop instead is also good, but far wilder: both the bishop on b5 and the pawn on e4 hang at the same time. } 10... Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Qa5+ 12. Nfd2 Qxb5 13. Nxd6 { This turns into a genuinely messy fight. Taking with the knight on d6 instead keeps the bishop safely at home rather than hanging in the open. }) (10. O-O { Declining the pawn is possible too. Castling first keeps development simple, though it lets him keep the extra tempo the offer bought him. } 10... b4 11. Na4 Ba6 { His pawns roll forward while your knight sits stuck on the rim of the board. Taking the pawn on move ten avoids this entirely. [%csl Ra4] }) (10. a4 { Attacking the pawn with a4 only forces him to push it one square further, and now it is your own knight that has nowhere useful to go. } 10... b4 11. Ne2 Re8 { His pawns and pieces flow forward together now, and the pawn on e4 has become a real target for the knight sitting on f6. [%csl Rb4][%cal Rf6e4] }) (10. Nd2 { Retreating the knight to d2 is passive. It blocks its own bishop on d3 and does nothing at all about the pawn he just offered on b5. } 10... c4 11. Bc2 b4 { The knight gets chased right off the board, and by the time it finds a square his pawns already own the entire queen side. }) (10. e5 { Breaking open the centre with e5 comes a move too early. The knight on c3 has not even captured the free pawn on b5 yet. } 10... dxe5 11. Bxb5 e4 { His pawn push to e4 hits the knight on f3, and the position settles back to level at best, no edge left from the earlier pawn sacrifice. }) 10... Re8 { This is his best reply: the rook eyes the pawn on e4 straight away. Castle and let the pawn hang. A rook coming to e1 will pin whatever takes it. [%cal Ye1g1] } 11. O-O { Castling tucks the king away first. If he grabs the pawn on e4 now, the rook lands on e1 next and pins whichever knight took it, against his own rook on e8. } (11. Nc3 { Retreating the knight to c3 instead of castling still lets him take the pawn on e4 anyway, since nothing else was guarding it. } 11... Nxe4 12. Nxe4 f5 { His pawn push to f5 wins the knight's square back with tempo, and the whole point of the sacrifice is gone. Castling first avoids handing him this. }) (11. Bg5 { Not yet, castle first. With the king still stuck on e1, his rook can swing all the way to e4 and arrive with check. } 11... c4 12. Bxc4 Rxe4+ { His rook lands on e4 with check, exactly the tactic that castling on the previous move would have prevented entirely. [%cal Re4e1] }) (11. Nd2 { Developing the knight to d2 is also good. From there it heads for c4, where it leans on the permanently weak pawn on d6. } 11... Nxd5 12. Nc4 { He grabs the pawn on d5 with his own knight, and yours lands on c4 to lean on d6 in return. A fair fight, just not the one this repertoire's main line reaches. }) (11. Nxd6 { Capturing the pawn on d6 looks tempting, but his queen on d8 guards it directly and simply recaptures. } 11... Qxd6 { His queen recaptures the knight. Between the pawn already won on b5 and this one on d6, the knight only bought two pawns, not a good trade. [%cal Rd8d6] }) 11... Nxe4 { He grabs the pawn on e4. Do not rush to recapture. Bring the rook to e1 first, and his knight is pinned to his own rook sitting on e8. [%cal Yf1e1] } 12. Re1 { The rook lands on e1, pinning the knight on e4 against his own rook behind it on e8. Nothing on the board defends that knight a second time. } (12. Bxe4 { Taking the knight with the bishop first is also fine. It recaptures immediately and chases his rook off the e column in the process. } 12... Rxe4 13. Bg5 Qd7 14. Nc3 Re8 { Material is level here, and this is perfectly playable, but keeping the pin with the rook on e1 leaves far more tension in the position for him to solve. }) (12. Nd2 { Offering the trade with the knight to d2 removes his knight from e4 at once, but along with it goes all the pressure this whole line was built around. } 12... Nxd2 13. Bxd2 a6 { The position is dead level here, and with both minor pieces traded off, his remaining bishops are perfectly happy with the open board left behind. }) (12. Nxd6 { Capturing on d6 looks free, but his knight, still sitting on e4, defends that very square and simply recaptures. } 12... Nxd6 { His knight recaptures on d6, and the trade really is just a knight for a single pawn, a bad bargain with nothing to show for it. [%cal Re4d6] }) 12... a6 { The pawn push to a6 kicks your knight away. Retreat it to c3, where it joins the attack on his pinned knight sitting on e4. [%cal Yb5c3] } 13. Nc3 { The knight rejoins the attack on e4. Three of your pieces now bear down on that pinned knight, and only his own rook on e8 stands behind it. } (13. Na3 { Retreating to a3 also saves the knight, though it sits on the rim of the board there, further from the action than c3 would be. } 13... Nf6 14. Rxe8+ Nxe8 15. Bg5 { This is playable: the knight can still work its way back into the game via c4 a few moves later. }) (13. Rxe4 { Taking the knight on e4 with the rook straight away is another option, exchanging the pin for the piece it was aimed at. } 13... Rxe4 14. Nxd6 Rb4 { The position stays messy but roughly level here. Retreating the knight to c3 instead keeps things far simpler to play. }) (13. Ng5 { Jumping the knight to g5 adds a second attacker on e4, but it leaves your own knight on b5 hanging and the pawn on f2 loose at the same time. } 13... Nxf2 { His knight grabs the loose pawn on f2, and the mess that follows is no longer in your favour. Retreating the knight to c3 avoids the whole tangle. }) (13. Bxe4 { Capturing with the bishop wins his knight outright, but your own knight on b5 is left hanging in return, so nothing has really been gained. } 13... axb5 { He recaptures on b5 and the material is level again, but his pawn structure is the healthier one. Retreating the knight to c3 first avoids this trade. }) 13... Nxc3 { He trades the knight off. Capture on e8 with check first, and only then take back on c3. The order genuinely matters here. [%cal Ye1e8] } 14. Rxe8+ { The rook takes on e8 with check, so he has to deal with that first. The knight sitting on c3 has to wait its turn. } (14. bxc3 { Recapturing with the pawn first works just as well here, since his rook on e8 has nowhere useful to run before you take it anyway. } 14... Bxc3 15. Rxe8+ Qxe8 16. Rb1 { He grabs the pawn on c3 with his bishop, but the open b column and a real lead in development are worth at least that much in return. }) (14. Qd2 { Stepping the queen out to d2 attacks the knight on c3, but it simply steps back to safety, and you are down a whole piece for nothing. } 14... Nb5 { The knight escapes to b5 with the piece still in hand, a whole knight down, since it was never recaptured. [%cal Rc3b5] }) (14. Bg5 { Developing the bishop to g5 ignores the real problem: his knight, still sitting on c3, is attacking your queen on d1. } 14... Nxd1 { His knight takes the queen before you can move her, the cost of leaving that knight on c3 unanswered for even one more move. [%cal Rc3d1] }) 14... Qxe8 { Now recapture on c3 with the pawn. Pawns on c3 and d5 support each other in a chain, and neither one can be won for free. [%cal Yb2c3] } 15. bxc3 { Material is level again, his b pawn for your e pawn, and the open b column, with nothing black left to contest it, is entirely yours. [%csl Gb1] } (15. Qd2 { Attacking the knight on c3 with the queen here just lets it run to d5, grabbing the wedge pawn on the way out. } 15... Nxd5 { The knight escapes with the pawn on d5 in hand, a pawn down, and the wedge this whole opening was built around has simply vanished. [%cal Rc3d5] }) (15. Qb3 { The same story as before: attacking the knight on c3 from b3 just lets it step calmly away, and nothing has been won for the trouble. } 15... Nb5 { The knight reaches safety on b5, a whole piece to the good for him, since it was captured on c3 and never recaptured. [%cal Rc3b5] }) 15... Nd7 { His knight heads for e5. Swing the rook to b1: the open column points straight through the pawn on b7 at everything sitting behind it. [%cal Ya1b1] } 16. Rb1 { The rook lands on the b column with nothing black owns to block it, taking aim through the pawn on b7 at the pieces waiting behind it. } (16. Bd2 { Guarding the pawn on c3 with the bishop first is also good, and it removes every trick he finds against the tries below. } 16... Bb7 17. Qb3 { This is fine chess too. The rook simply arrives on the open b column one move later than in the main line. }) (16. Bf4 { The bishop pressures the pawn on d6, but leaves the pawn on c3 completely undefended in the process. } 16... Bxc3 17. Rc1 Be5 { His bishop grabs the pawn on c3 and retreats to e5 with tempo on the rook, a clean pawn won for nothing given back. [%cal Rg7c3] }) (16. Bg5 { The same problem as developing the bishop to f4: it ignores the pawn on c3 completely, and he simply takes it. } 16... Bxc3 17. Rb1 Ne5 { A pawn is gone, and it is his pieces, not yours, doing all the active work on the board now. }) (16. Ba3 { The bishop eyes the pawn on d6 from a3, but once again it does nothing to guard the pawn on c3, which simply falls. } 16... Bxc3 17. Rb1 Ne5 { A pawn down again: every bishop move that skips guarding c3 loses that same pawn in exactly the same way. [%cal Rg7c3] }) 16... Ne5 { He offers to trade knights on e5. Take it. That knight was his single best piece, and it was already attacking your bishop on d3. [%cal Yf3e5] } 17. Nxe5 { Trading it off is the safest answer. It removes the knight before it can fork your own knight and bishop with a single jump. } (17. Be4 { Stepping the bishop aside to e4 does not save the trade. His knight simply captures the knight on f3 anyway. } 17... Nxf3+ 18. Bxf3 Bxc3 { With the knight gone from f3, nothing guards the pawn on c3 any longer, and his bishop collects it for free. [%cal Rg7c3] }) (17. Bc2 { Hiding the bishop on c2 does not stop the trade either. The knight still takes on f3, and his queen pours into the open position that follows. } 17... Nxf3+ 18. Qxf3 Qe1+ 19. Kh2 Be5+ { His string of checks drives your king around and wins the pawn on c3 as the final prize, all because the trade on e5 was declined. [%cal Re1e5] }) (17. Nd2 { Retreating the knight to d2 blocks your own queen from the d column and still does nothing to stop the knight sitting on e5. } 17... Nxd3 { His knight simply takes the bishop on d3, a whole piece lost for a retreat that never addressed the real threat. [%cal Re5d3] }) 17... Bxe5 { Now bring the bishop to d2: it guards the pawn on c3 at last, freeing your queen and rook to work the open b column undisturbed. [%cal Yc1d2] } 18. Bd2 { Material is level. You hold the open b column and the wedge on d5 against his permanently weak pawn on d6; his side of the ledger is the bishop pair. [%csl Rd6][%cal Gb1b7] } (18. Qb3 { Bringing the queen to b3 first is also good: queen and rook now double up on the open b column together. } 18... Ra7 19. Qb8 { This is playable, though developing the bishop to d2 first, before the queen, keeps the pawn on c3 permanently out of danger. }) (18. c4 { Fixing the pawn on c4 is playable too. It is safe there and no longer needs the bishop's protection at all. } 18... Bd7 19. Bh6 { Material stays level, but with the pawns locked this way, the d4 square belongs to him permanently. Nothing of yours can ever contest it again. [%csl Rd4] }) (18. Bh6 { Developing the bishop to h6 is active, aiming straight at his king, but it leaves the pawn on c3 completely undefended once again. } 18... Bxc3 19. Rb6 Be5 { A pawn down, the same cost every bishop move that ignores c3 has paid throughout this whole line. [%cal Re5c3] }) (18. Bg5 { The same problem yet again: developing the bishop to g5 leaves the pawn on c3 hanging, and it falls for a second time. } 18... Bxc3 19. Qf3 Bd4 { A pawn down again, and this time his bishop has also found a strong post on d4 to sit on. [%cal Re5c3] }) (18. Bf4 { Developing the bishop to f4 walks it straight onto a square nothing defends. His bishop on e5 simply takes it. } 18... Bxf4 { His bishop captures on f4 for free, a whole piece lost this time, the worst of every try that skipped guarding c3. [%cal Re5f4] }) 1-0


[Event "Benoni: he takes on e4 as well"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/3Ll0yprL/SJt6oaI0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "rnbq1rk1/p4pbp/3p1np1/1NpP4/4P3/3B1N1P/PP3PP1/R1BQK2R b KQ - 0 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbq1rk1/p4pbp/3p1np1/1NpP4/4P3/3B1N1P/PP3PP1/R1BQK2R_b_KQ_-_0_10"]

{ This Benoni line already has you a pawn up. He now grabs a second pawn on e4 with his knight, opening lines for counterplay. It is not sound, but every reply needs a precise answer. }
10... Nxe4 { The knight lands on e4 and grabs a second pawn, eyeing your pawn on f2 next. What do you play? } 11. Bxe4 { Simplest: the bishop recaptures the knight at once. His rook will swing to e8 next and pin your bishop to the king along the e column. } (11. O-O { Castling looks calm, but it lets his knight on e4 escape home instead of losing it to your bishop. } 11... a6 12. Na3 Nf6 { He first kicks your knight on b5 with the a pawn, then brings his own knight home to f6. Material is level, when the piece was there for the taking on e4. [%cal Ga6b5] }) (11. Nxd6 { Grabbing the pawn on d6 looks tempting, but his queen guards it and simply recaptures. } 11... Nxd6 { A whole knight given for one pawn: his queen recaptures at once, and you are down a full piece for far too little. [%cal Re4d6] }) (11. Nc3 { The knight retreats to c3, but his knight on e4 simply captures it there, and your position falls apart. } 11... Nxc3 12. bxc3 Bxc3+ { His bishop swoops onto c3 with check, forking your king on e1 and your rook on a1 along the same diagonal. [%cal Rc3e1,Rc3a1] }) (11. Qe2 { The queen steps to e2, but that does nothing about the knight sitting on b5, and he attacks it at once with his a pawn. } 11... a6 12. Bxe4 axb5 { Material comes back level, and his queenside pawns start rolling. Simplest was to take the knight on e4 right away, not spend a move on the queen. }) 11... Re8 { His rook swings to e8 and pins your bishop to the king along the e column, with nothing between them to block it. What still guards the bishop? } 12. Ng5 { The knight jumps to g5, guarding the pinned bishop on e4 while eyeing the weak squares f7 and e6. His h pawn will come to kick it next move. } (12. Nd2 { The knight on d2 also defends the bishop and is playable, but the knight on g5 bites into f7 and e6 too, so it is the sharper choice. } 12... Ba6 13. a4 { A slower fight than the main line, but you still hold the extra piece for now. }) (12. O-O { Castling here simply lets his rook capture the bishop on e4 next move, handing the piece straight back. } 12... Rxe4 13. Bg5 Qd7 { Material is level again and his pieces are active. The knight jump to g5, instead of castling, was what kept the whole piece. }) (12. Qc2 { The queen adds a defender to the pawn on e4, but he can swing his bishop to f5 and attack it a second time. } 12... Bf5 13. Ng5 Bxe4 14. Nxe4 f5 { His bishop trades itself for yours on e4, but your knight recaptures at once and you are still a full piece up. }) (12. Qe2 { The queen guards the bishop, but he can swing his own bishop to a6, pinning your knight on b5 to the queen along that diagonal. } 12... Ba6 13. a4 Qa5+ { The pin has real teeth now: his queen checks, and the knight on b5 cannot be saved for long, while your king still sits in the centre. }) 12... h6 { He kicks the knight on g5 with his h pawn. Does it retreat home, or jump forward into his camp? } 13. Ne6 { Into his camp: if his f pawn takes the knight, your d pawn recaptures on e6, and your bishop on e4 suddenly stares straight down at his rook on a8. [%cal Ge4a8] } (13. Nf3 { Retreating the knight home to f3 abandons the bishop on e4 completely: nothing still guards it. } 13... Rxe4 { Check! With the knight gone from g5, nothing still guards the bishop on e4, and his rook simply collects it. [%cal Re8e4] }) (13. Nxf7 { The hit on f7 looks tempting, but it gives away the pin on e4 for nothing solid in return. } 13... Rxe4+ 14. Kf1 Kxf7 { Check, and then his king simply collects the stranded knight on f7 too. You have given back the whole advantage for nothing. }) (13. O-O { Castling ignores the attacked knight on g5 completely, and his h pawn simply collects it next move. } 13... hxg5 14. Bc2 { A whole knight gone for nothing concrete: material falls back to level, and the bishop retreat to c2 is all you get for it. }) 13... Qa5+ { Check! Along the same row his queen also attacks the knight on b5. Every legal block must be counted. } 14. Nc3 { The only block that saves everything: the knight comes home from b5 to c3, blocking the check. His fianchetto bishop already attacks that square, so a trade follows. } (14. Bd2 { This block does nothing for the knight on b5, and it simply falls next. } 14... Qxb5 { Gone. Nothing guarded the knight on b5, so his queen simply collects it. [%cal Ra5b5] }) (14. Qd2 { This block does nothing for the knight on b5 either: the queen ignores it completely, and it falls next. } 14... Qxb5 { The knight on b5 was still undefended, and his queen takes it for free. [%cal Ra5b5] }) (14. Kf1 { The king sidesteps the check, but the knight on b5 falls anyway, with check. } 14... Qxb5+ 15. Bd3 Qxd3+ 16. Qxd3 Ba6 { His queen recaptures the bishop, but walks straight into yours: he loses the queen for a bishop, and you come out clearly ahead. Block with the knight instead, for a calmer path to the same edge. }) 14... Bxc3+ { His bishop takes the knight with check. Recapture at once. } 15. bxc3 { The pawn recaptures, walking into a check from his queen, but you are still a piece up and nothing worse follows. } (15. Bd2 { A block, but every piece trades off from here, and your knight on e6 is left with no defender at the end of it. } 15... Bxd2+ 16. Qxd2 Qxd2+ 17. Kxd2 fxe6 { The knight on e6 falls for nothing in return: a whole piece down. }) (15. Kf1 { The king steps aside, out of the check, but your rook still sleeps on h1, undeveloped. } 15... Be5 { His bishop rules the long diagonal from e5, and your king on f1 has nowhere good to go. [%csl Rf1] }) 15... Qxc3+ { Check again, and this time the rook on a1 hangs too. Block the check. } 16. Bd2 { The bishop blocks on d2 with tempo: from there it attacks his queen on c3 directly. } (16. Qd2 { Trading queens on d2 is possible, but it gives up the attack for no real gain. } 16... Qxd2+ 17. Kxd2 fxe6 { Trading queens here leaves you only a single pawn down, far cheaper than losing the knight outright, but the bishop block on d2 keeps far more of your advantage than this does. }) (16. Kf1 { The king steps out of check, but the rook on a1 has no defender and falls next. } 16... Qxa1 { A whole rook, undefended on a1 and simply taken. [%cal Rc3a1] }) (16. Ke2 { The king walks toward the open board instead of away from it, straight into the fire. } 16... Ba6+ { Check, and with the king out in the open the rook on a1 falls next too. [%cal Ra6e2] }) 16... Qc4 { His queen swings to c4 and attacks your bishop on e4 along the fourth row. What is the cleanest answer? } 17. Qe2 { Offer the queen trade: your queen and rook already have him tied down, so simplifying keeps every ounce of the advantage. } (17. Qf3 { Also good: the queen defends the bishop on e4 from f3, and from there it hits f7 too. } 17... Bxe6 18. Rc1 { His queen is exposed too, and the fight stays roughly balanced with a small pull your way. }) (17. Bf3 { The bishop steps back to f3; playable, and it keeps guarding e4 from the side. } 17... fxe6 { He takes the stranded knight on e6, and your d pawn recaptures next. A smaller edge than keeping the piece outright. }) (17. Qc2 { The other trade offer, swapping queens on c2 instead of e2. } 17... Qxc2 18. Bxc2 fxe6 { Trading queens here is also playable, but after he takes the stranded knight on e6, your edge shrinks to little more than a shade of a pawn. }) (17. Nc7 { The knight jump to c7 forks both his rooks, but his rook on e8 takes your bishop with check first, and the fork is refuted before it starts. } 17... Rxe4+ { Check, and with the bishop gone the fork on c7 has nothing left to win. [%cal Re8e4] }) (17. Nxc5 { The knight grabs the pawn on c5, but it has no follow-up and simply gets recaptured next. } 17... dxc5 { His pawn recaptures the knight: the whole extra piece is gone, and material is level again. [%cal Rd6c5] }) (17. Bxg6 { The bishop grabs the pawn on g6, but the sacrifice does not work: too many pieces come off along the way. } 17... Bxe6 18. dxe6 fxg6 { After all the trades on e6 and g6, you have given up a bishop for a single pawn, and the advantage is gone for nothing. }) 17... Qxe2+ { He offers to trade queens with check: simplifying is his best practical try while down material. } 18. Kxe2 { The king steps in to recapture on e2. It looks bold out in the open, but nothing of his can reach it yet, and the knight on e6 still needs sorting out. [%tail] } 18... fxe6 19. dxe6 19... Rxe6 20. Kf3 20... Rxe4 21. Kxe4 21... Bb7+ 22. Kf4 22... Bxg2 23. Rhg1 { The rook swings to g1, facing the bishop down. Material is level for now, but his rook and knight have never left the back rank, while every piece you own is doing real work. [%cal Gg1g2] } 1-0


