12... Qa5+ { Check! His queen on a5 attacks the knight on b5 too, both sitting on the fifth row. Every legal block must be counted. } 13. Nc3 { The one block that saves the knight: it comes home from b5 to c3, blocking the check. His fianchetto bishop already eyes that square, so a trade follows. } (13. Kf1 { The king steps out of the check, but the knight on b5 falls too, and with check. } 13... Qxb5+ 14. Kg1 Nd7 { Level again: the whole extra piece is gone, and your rook still sleeps on h1, boxed in behind your own king. [%csl Rh1] }) (13. Qd2 { The queen blocks the check on d2, but does nothing for the knight on b5, so it is lost too. } 13... Qxb5 { Gone. The queen on d2 blocked the check but ignored b5 completely, so his queen simply collects the knight there. [%cal Ra5b5] }) (13. Bd2 { The bishop blocks the check on d2, but it too does nothing for the knight on b5. } 13... Qxb5 { Gone. The bishop on d2 blocked the check the same way and still left b5 unguarded, so the knight falls too. [%cal Ra5b5] }) (13. Ke2 { The king steps into the open board instead of blocking with a piece. } 13... Qxb5+ { Check, and material is level again: the whole extra piece is given back to save the king. [%cal Rb5e2] }) 13... h6 { Now the kick with his h pawn. Where does the knight go? The knight on c3 already guards your bishop on e4, so count carefully. } 14. Ne6 { Forward, into his camp: the knight eyes the bishop on g7, and if the f pawn ever takes it, your d pawn recaptures on e6 and your bishop on e4 aims straight at the rook on a8. [%csl Ge6] } (14. Nf3 { Back home to f3: the bishop on e4 is guarded again, but watch the long diagonal, where his fianchetto bishop still aims through c3. } 14... Bxc3+ 15. bxc3 Qxc3+ 16. Bd2 Rxe4+ { Check! His rook picks up the bishop on e4 anyway, and material tips slightly his way. [%cal Re8e4] }) (14. Nxf7 { The hit on f7 comes too early: it gives away the pin on e4 before it has done any real work. } 14... Bxc3+ 15. bxc3 Rxe4+ { Check, and next he simply captures the stranded knight on f7 with his king. The whole advantage is gone for nothing. [%cal Re8e4] }) (14. O-O { Castling gives back the knight for two pawns and a safer king: a real option, but a smaller one. } 14... hxg5 15. Bc2 Qd8 { Roughly level material. The knight jump to e6, instead of castling, was what kept the whole extra piece. }) (14. Bd2 { A quiet move that does nothing about the attacked knight on g5, and he simply takes it next. } 14... hxg5 15. O-O Qd8 { Two pawns for the piece, and material comes back level at best. }) 1-0


