{ Your knight sits on g5 in this Benoni, and your bishop on e4 is pinned to your king along the e column. } 12... f5 { His pawn jumps to f5, hitting the pinned bishop on e4. The pin is real, but one move breaks it outright — find it. } 13. O-O { Castling: the king steps off the column, the bishop is free, and the whole e column now belongs to your rook. } (13. Qb3 { The queen steps off the column too, but the bishop still just falls to the pawn. } 13... fxe4 14. O-O a6 15. Nc3 { Still winning for you, but his pawn reached e4 with a free tempo. Castling straight away avoids that. }) (13. Qa4 { This attacks his rook on e8, but it does nothing about the pin on your own bishop. } 13... Bd7 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Rxe6 { His pawn on e6 falls too, but the whole exchange leaves you a clean two pawns ahead — good, just less than castling keeps. }) (13. a4 { Far too slow: the bishop on e4 stays pinned to your king, and fxe4 simply wins it next move. } 13... fxe4 14. O-O h6 { He wins the bishop clean and keeps every pawn he has. Material is level — castling first was the whole point. }) (13. Qc2 { The queen adds a guard to the bishop, but a check is coming right back at you. } 13... Qa5+ 14. Nc3 Qa6 { You are still a clean piece up on the scoreboard, but your king is stuck in the centre and the bishop is still pinned — real trouble despite the material. }) (13. Qe2 { The queen guards the bishop, but the pin along the column never goes away. } 13... fxe4 14. O-O e3 { Material is level, and his advanced pawn on e3 is a permanent thorn in your position. }) (13. Ne6 { The fork square, but one move too early — the pin on your own bishop is still there to be exploited. } 13... Qa5+ 14. Nc3 Bxe6 { He takes the knight with the check still in hand. At best this is level material. }) (13. Qf3 { The queen guards the bishop, but he simply kicks the knight next. } 13... h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Rxe6 { His rook mops up the pawn on e6, but the whole sequence still leaves you a clear two pawns ahead — the pin never went away first, though. }) (13. Nxd6 { The pawn on d6 was guarded by his queen the whole time. } 13... Qxd6 { His queen simply recaptures, so a whole knight is gone for one pawn. [%cal Rd8d6] }) 13... fxe4 { He takes the bishop's pawn. Work out which piece attacks the pawn on e4 now. } 14. Re1 { The rook joins the open column: the pawn on e4 is attacked, and the pin that was once yours to fear now works against him. } (14. Bf4 { Also good, hitting the weak pawn on d6 first instead. } 14... Be5 15. Qd2 Bf5 16. Bxe5 Rxe5 { A fine position for you, but bringing the rook to e1 is the sharper way to press the pawn on e4. }) (14. Qb3 { The queen comes out, but it gives him a free move to organise. } 14... a6 15. Nc3 Bf5 { About a pawn better for you; the rook to e1 wins back more of the centre. }) (14. Ne6 { The fork square again, and again too early — the pawn on e4 is not yet won. } 14... Bxe6 15. dxe6 Na6 { Only half of what the rook move gives you. }) (14. Nxe4 { The knight grabs the pawn and simply hangs there. } 14... Rxe4 { Nothing at all guards the knight on e4, so the rook just collects it. [%cal Re8e4] }) (14. Nc3 { The knight comes home, but the pawn on e4 stands firm and unattacked. } 14... Bf5 { His pawn on e4 is safe now, and he is doing fine out of the opening. }) 14... Bf5 { He guards the pawn on e4 with his bishop. One of your own pieces has still not moved at all — bring it in. } 15. Bf4 { The bishop hits the weak pawn on d6. He has to guard it with his own bishop, pulling it clean off the long diagonal at g7. } (15. g4 { Also strong: the pawn simply kicks his bishop off f5. } 15... a6 16. Nc3 { Still winning for you, but sharper and riskier than it needs to be. }) (15. Ne6 { The knight jumps into his camp at once. } 15... Bxe6 16. dxe6 d5 { You keep an edge, but the pawn on e6 falls soon after — less than the bishop move keeps. }) (15. Nc3 { The knight comes back to add a second attacker on e4. } 15... Bxc3 16. bxc3 { Half your edge disappears into doubled pawns on the c column. }) (15. Nxe4 { Still not: his bishop and rook both watch the pawn on e4 twice over, so grabbing it there costs you the exchange. } 15... Bxe4 16. Rxe4 Rxe4 { A whole piece is gone for your trouble. [%cal Re8e4] }) (15. Rxe4 { The rook grabs the pawn, and his bishop simply takes the rook back. } 15... Bxe4 16. Nxe4 { The knight recaptures the bishop, but his rook on e8 still eyes the knight on e4 — let that fall too and a whole rook is gone for nothing. }) 15... Be5 { His bishop comes to guard the pawn on d6. Count what is happening on e4 right now. } 16. Nxe4 { The knight takes the pawn on e4. It is guarded by your rook, and from there it eyes both f6 and d6. } (16. Qd2 { Slower: it lets him untangle with the knight to d7 before you win the pawn back, so your edge shrinks to just one pawn. } 16... Nd7 17. Nxe4 Bxe4 18. Rxe4 { Still good for you, but he has finished developing in the meantime. }) (16. Bxe5 { Trading bishops only helps him simplify toward safety. } 16... Rxe5 17. f4 exf3 18. Nxf3 { A smaller edge than keeping the bishops on the board. }) (16. Rxe4 { The rook grabs the pawn, and the bishop simply takes it back. } 16... Bxe4 17. Nxe4 { You come out of this the exchange down, and his bishop is about to take on f4 as well. }) (16. Nc3 { The knight comes back and forgets all about the bishop on e5. } 16... Bxf4 { A whole bishop is gone for nothing. [%cal Re5f4] }) 16... Bxf4 { He takes your bishop on f4 and expects the automatic recapture. Look for something sharper first. } 17. Nf6+ { Check, and a fork at the same time: the rook on e8 stands right behind the king on the same diagonal. He has to take with the queen. } (17. Qf3 { The queen hits both bishops at once, but he simply takes the knight and untangles. } 17... Rxe4 18. Rxe4 Be5 { Material swings his way, and you come out of this worse off. }) (17. Nbxd6 { The other knight grabs the pawn on d6 instead of forking. } 17... Bxd6 { His bishop simply recaptures it. [%cal Rf4d6] }) (17. Nc7 { The fork on a8 and e8 arrives a move too late to matter here. } 17... Rxe4 18. Rxe4 Bxe4 { He gets to take two of your pieces before you take anything back. }) (17. Ng3 { The knight runs away and leaves everything else hanging behind it. } 17... Rxe1+ 18. Qxe1 Bd7 { You finish a whole piece down. }) 17... Qxf6 { His queen recaptures the knight. Now his own rook on e8 is left completely undefended. } 18. Rxe8+ { Check, and the rook on e8 was undefended the whole time: it falls for free while his king still sits stuck in the centre. } (18. Nc7 { The other fork, arriving one move too late to matter. } 18... Rc8 { His rook on e8 takes yours next, and your knight on c7 has nowhere safe to run. This is lost. }) (18. Qf3 { This does nothing: the rook on e8 was there for the taking, and this move lets it live while he regroups. } 18... Be5 { His two bishops now rule the board, and your rook on e1 is the one that falls. This is lost. }) (18. g4 { Kicking the bishop here lets the whole position slip away. } 18... Nd7 { His pieces flood into the game, and you are lost. }) 18... Kf7 { His king steps up and attacks your rook directly. Save it, and count what you are left with. } 19. Qe1 { The queen guards the rook, which in turn hits his stranded knight on b8. Material comes out dead level — the exchange, a rook for a bishop, against one of his pawns — but his king still has no shelter at all. } (19. Nc7 { Also fine: this brings the fork on the rook at a8 instead. } 19... Qxb2 20. Re2 { The material count looks close, but your attack is still clearly the stronger side of this position. }) (19. Qe2 { This also guards the rook, just from a different square. } 19... a6 20. Nc7 { Fine too: the knight still jumps to c7 and forks the rook on a8, the same trick the other move order gives you. }) (19. Qa4 { The queen wanders off and leaves the rook to fend for itself. } 19... Bd7 20. Re6 { You keep an edge, but a smaller one than guarding the rook directly. }) (19. Qf3 { This gives the rook back for nothing extra. } 19... Kxe8 20. Qxf4 { You win the bishop back, but only after giving up a whole rook for it — you finish worse off, not a pawn up. }) (19. Re6 { The rook runs again, straight into another attack. } 19... Qg5 { A smaller edge: the rook keeps getting chased around while his queen reaches g5 with real threats near your own king. }) (19. Rxb8 { The rook grabs the stranded knight, then dies for it. } 19... Rxb8 { His rook recaptures, so you traded a whole rook for a single knight. [%cal Ra8b8] }) (19. Nxd6+ { The knight grabs the pawn with check, but the bishop simply takes it back. } 19... Bxd6 { And your rook on e8 is still hanging besides. [%cal Rf4d6] }) 1-0


