[Role "exercise"]

{ This is the Benoni. You have just captured the pawn on b5 with your knight. Now he recaptures on d5 with his own knight, hoping to even the pawns and open his bishop's diagonal from g7. Count the material before you decide. }
10... Nxd5 { His knight recaptures the pawn on d5. Which of your pieces can take it back? } 11. exd5 { Your pawn recaptures, and the knight is gone for good. The bishop on d3 guards the knight on b5 along that diagonal, so nothing hangs and you are a clean piece up. [%csl Gb5][%cal Gd3b5][%tail] } (11. O-O { Castling first leaves the knight on d5 alive, and your one shot at trapping it for free is gone. } 11... a6 12. Nxd6 Qxd6 13. Bc4 Bb7 { Right now you are down a knight for a pawn, but the knight on d5 still hangs to your pawn on e4. Grab it and you are only a pawn up, far less than the clean piece the direct capture wins. }) (11. Be2 { Developing the bishop quietly does nothing about the knight on d5, so it simply survives. } 11... Nc7 12. Nxd6 Nc6 { A pawn up only, and far less than you deserved. His knight hopped to safety on c7 before you could trap it. }) (11. Bg5 { The bishop attacks the queen on d8 from g5, forcing her to move, but the knight on d5 is left completely alone. } 11... Qa5+ 12. Qd2 Nb4 { Material is level. The knight escaped to d5 and now sits on b4, eyeing your bishop on d3, though your queen still guards it. Castle and you keep a small edge, but the clean piece from the main line is gone. }) (11. Nxd6 { Grabbing the pawn on d6 with the knight ignores the real prize, the knight already trapped on d5, and it costs you material instead. } 11... Qxd6 { The queen recaptures on d6, and your knight is gone for just a single pawn, a trade you never needed to make. [%cal Rd8d6] }) (11. Bc4 { The bishop attacks the knight on d5 from c4, but that only forces it to move somewhere. It does not win it outright. } 11... Nb6 { Material stays level. The knight hops to b6, attacking your bishop on c4, and the piece you were hoping to trap simply stays on the board. }) Qa5+ 12. Kf1 Ba6 13. a4 { Your a-pawn steps up to guard the knight on b5 a second time, alongside the bishop on d3. Nothing on b5 is going anywhere, and you remain a clean piece up. [%csl Gb5][%cal Ga4b5] } 1-0


