{ This is the Benoni. You have just captured the pawn on b5 with your knight. Now he kicks it with the a pawn, the natural human move, but it simply leaves him a clean pawn down. }
10... a6 { His pawn kicks the knight off b5. Which square keeps it doing useful work? } 11. Nc3 { The knight comes home to c3, where it guards both the e4 and d5 pawns at once. You are a clean pawn up, and his pawn on d6 is a weak point with only the queen behind it. } (11. Na3 { Tucking the knight away on a3 is the rim, and from there it guards nothing that matters in the centre. } 11... Re8 12. O-O Nxe4 { His knight grabs the pawn on e4, since the knight on a3 was doing nothing to defend it. Material is level again, and the extra pawn from taking on b5 is gone. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (11. Nxd6 { Grabbing the pawn on d6 walks the knight straight into the queen on d8, which still guards it along the open d column. } 11... Qxd6 12. e5 Re8 { You have given up a whole knight for two pawns, and his rook and queen are already active on the open lines. Worse than simply keeping the extra pawn from taking on b5. }) (11. Qa4 { The queen guards the knight on b5 from a4, but it does nothing about his c pawn, which is one push away from hitting the bishop on d3. } 11... c4 12. Nxd6 cxd3 { The pawn recaptures on d3, and your bishop is gone. Grabbing the pawn on d6 along the way was not worth losing a whole piece for it. [%cal Rc4d3] }) (11. Nbd4 { Jumping the knight to d4 lands it right on a square his own pawn on c5 already guards. } 11... cxd4 { The pawn simply takes the knight, and it is gone for nothing. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (11. e5 { Pushing the pawn to e5 straight away just hands it over, since nothing of yours is behind it yet. } 11... dxe5 12. Nc3 e4 { Material is level, but his pawn on e4 now attacks both your bishop on d3 and your knight on f3 at once. Much worse than simply keeping the extra pawn. }) 11... Nbd7 { He brings the other knight out to d7. Which square in his own camp is left without a real defender? } 12. Bf4 { The bishop swings to f4 and hits the pawn on d6, which the knight on d7 has just cut off from the queen. He has to bring the queen back round to guard it. } (12. O-O { Castling first is also fine here, since the pawn on d6 is not going anywhere before you get back to it. } 12... Nh5 13. Be2 { A pawn up, and the bishop steps out of the knight's reach on e2 with nothing else for him to grab. }) (12. Be3 { The bishop develops to e3, eyeing the pawn on c5, but that pawn is guarded twice and there is no real threat yet. } 12... Rb8 13. Rb1 { A pawn up either way, but the bishop asks more questions from f4, where it actually hits the pawn on d6. }) (12. Bd2 { Tucking the bishop onto d2 is passive, but it is still a perfectly sound square to develop from. } 12... Rb8 { Still simply a pawn up - his rook coming to b8 does nothing to change that. }) (12. Bg5 { Pinning the knight on f6 with the bishop on g5 invites the pawn to kick it straight back with h6. } 12... h6 13. Bf4 Qe7 { The bishop lands back on f4 and the queen comes to e7, the same set-up as the main line, just with his pawn already committed to h6. }) (12. e5 { Pushing the pawn to e5 here is too early, since your own pieces are not yet ready to back it up. } 12... dxe5 { His pawn recaptures on e5, guarded by the knight on d7, so there is nothing to win back. A pawn simply handed over for nothing. }) 12... Qe7 { The queen steps to e7, guarding the pawn on d6 and eyeing the one on e4 too. Time to get your own king safe. } 13. O-O { Your king tucks away on g1. His knight is about to jump to h5 and hit the bishop on f4 - let it come, there is a good square waiting. } (13. Bh2 { Retreating the bishop to h2 before his knight even threatens it just costs you a move for nothing yet. } 13... Rb8 { Still a pawn up, but you spent a tempo retreating a bishop nobody had even attacked yet, while he simply developed. }) (13. Qd2 { Bringing the queen to d2 first is fine too, and it gets ready to swing over toward the king side. } 13... Nh5 14. Bh6 Bxh6 15. Qxh6 Ne5 { Still a pawn up after the bishops came off, and now his knight lands on e5 looking for activity. }) (13. Qe2 { The queen guards the pawn on e4 from e2, but it also blocks your own bishop's retreat along the d3 to f1 diagonal. } 13... Nh5 14. Bh2 { Retreating the bishop here still keeps you ahead, but it is only about half the edge that castling first would have kept. }) (13. Be3 { The bishop steps back to e3, off the pawn on d6, but the pawn on e4 is not actually hanging - the knight on c3 and the bishop on d3 both guard it. } 13... Nxe4 14. Nxe4 c4 { He sacrifices the knight for the pawn anyway, and after your knight recaptures, the pawn on c4 attacks the bishop on d3. You are still a piece up this moment, but let the bishop fall and it drops to just a pawn up. [%cal Rc4d3] }) (13. Bxd6 { Grabbing the pawn on d6 again runs straight into the queen on e7, which still guards it. } 13... Qxd6 { The queen recaptures on d6, and your bishop is gone for a single pawn. [%cal Re7d6] }) (13. e5 { Pushing to e5 here loses the pawn again - his pawn on d6 simply recaptures, and nothing of yours is ready to contest e5. } 13... dxe5 { His pawn retakes on e5, guarded now by both the knight on d7 and the queen on e7. A pawn handed back for nothing. }) 13... Nh5 { His knight jumps to h5 and hits the bishop on f4. Which square keeps it safe and still useful? } 14. Bh2 { The bishop tucks into h2, where it still eyes the pawn on d6 and no black pawn can ever kick it out. } (14. Bg5 { The bishop jumps to g5 to hit the queen, but his pawn just kicks it straight back with f6. } 14... f6 15. Bc1 { Still a pawn up, but the bishop has now spent two moves just to end up back on c1, doing nothing at all. }) (14. Be3 { The bishop can also drop back to e3. If his knight then jumps to e5, you simply trade it off with the knight on f3 and stay a pawn up. } 14... Ne5 15. Nxe5 Bxe5 { Still a pawn up after the trade on e5, and his last minor piece there is gone too. }) (14. Bd2 { Dropping the bishop to d2 is fine too, but from there it no longer hits the pawn on d6 at all. } 14... Rb8 15. Rb1 { Simply a pawn up still, and the rook adds real pressure down the b column. }) (14. Bc1 { Retreating the bishop all the way home to c1 gives up on the d6 pawn completely, for no real gain. } 14... Ne5 { His knight jumps into e5 with real activity, and sending the bishop all the way home costs you about half the edge you had. }) (14. Qc1 { The queen steps to c1 to guard the bishop on f4, so he simply trades it off instead. } 14... Nxf4 15. Qxf4 Ne5 { His knight lands on e5 with tempo, forking your bishop on d3 and your knight on f3. You are still a pawn up, but this pressure buys back most of that edge. }) (14. Bg3 { The bishop stands its ground on g3, so he simply takes it, and your pawns end up doubled after you recapture. } 14... Nxg3 15. fxg3 { Worse. Taking back with the pawn leaves you doubled on the g column and strips the shelter in front of the king you just castled. }) (14. Bxd6 { The bishop takes the pawn on d6 here too, but the queen on e7 guards it, and the piece is simply lost for a pawn. } 14... Qxd6 { The queen takes back on d6, and once again your bishop is gone for a single pawn. [%cal Re7d6] }) 14... Ne5 { His other knight lands on e5, hitting both your bishop and your knight at once. Do you step one of them back, or trade it off? } 15. Be2 { The bishop tucks safely back to e2, out of the knight's reach, keeping the pawn edge intact while his knight on h5 still has nowhere useful to go. [%tail] } (15. Nxe5 { Trading the knight off on e5 is fine too, since the bishop recaptures and you simply keep the extra pawn. } 15... Bxe5 { Still just a pawn up after the trade, with nothing left for him to grab hold of. }) (15. Re1 { The rook can also swing to e1 and join the centre. The knight on e5 has no real threat here, since both the bishop on d3 and the knight on f3 are guarded. } 15... Rb8 { Still a pawn up regardless, and his rook landing on b8 does not challenge it either. }) (15. Rb1 { Slow. It does nothing about the knight sitting on e5, and it lets him follow up with f5, rolling his pawns forward. } 15... f5 { His pawns start rolling forward with f5, and the edge you keep here is smaller than elsewhere. }) (15. Bxe5 { Trading the dark-squared bishop for the knight on e5 gives up your bishop pair for no real gain. } 15... Bxe5 16. Nxe5 Qxe5 { Still a pawn up, but with the bishops traded off his own light-squared bishop gets more freedom than it should. Less convincing than keeping the piece. }) (15. Qd2 { The queen steps to d2, but that leaves the knight on f3 to be taken with check. } 15... Nxf3+ 16. gxf3 { Recapturing with the pawn is forced, and it tears open the pawn shield right in front of your own king. Worse than the tidy version elsewhere. [%csl Rg1] }) (15. Nd2 { Blocking with the knight on d2 looks natural, but it cuts the queen's own line to d3, and the bishop there is simply lost. } 15... Nxd3 { The knight simply takes the bishop on d3 for free, since the knight on d2 had blocked the only piece that was guarding it. [%cal Re5d3] }) 15... Nxf3+ 16. Bxf3 16... Rb8 17. Bxh5 17... gxh5 18. Qd2 { The queen steps up to d2, eyeing the weak pawn on h5 that nothing defends. Both his knights are gone, you are still a clean pawn up, and his king side pawns are broken wide open. [%csl Rh5] } 1-0


