{ He starts with the pawn to a6, planning a quiet b5 push with no gambit behind it. You already know this whole set-up, so play it straight from memory. } 9... a6 { His pawn steps to a6, getting ready to push b5 next move. Which pawn of yours stops b5 before it even starts? } 10. a4 { Right, the pawn to a4. A b5 push would simply hand him the pawn now, so his whole queenside plan is stuck before it begins. } ( 10. Bf4 { The bishop develops to f4, a natural move, but it does nothing at all about the b5 break that is coming. } b5 11. O-O Qe7 { He gets b5 for free and his queenside pawns start rolling forward. Playable chess, but there was no need to allow it. } ) ( 10. O-O { Castling straight away also lets the b5 push come through with nothing to stop it. } b5 11. Bf4 Qe7 { Once again b5 arrives completely unopposed, and grabbing that queenside space is his whole plan in this entire line. } ) ( 10. a3 { The pawn only reaches a3, one square short of the one that actually stops anything. } Re8 11. O-O b5 { The pawn on a3 never even touches b5. That move only cost you a whole tempo for nothing. } ) ( 10. Bg5 { The bishop pins the knight on f6 to his queen, but that pin does nothing at all to stop the b5 break. } b5 11. O-O Re8 { He gets his pawn to b5 and his rook onto the e column both for free. Comfortable chess for him. } ) ( 10. Be3 { The bishop sits loose on e3 here, with nothing at all pointing at d6. } b5 11. O-O Nbd7 { Once again b5 comes for free, and the bishop on e3 will become a target for his knight later on. } ) ( 10. e5 { The break with the e pawn is, once again, far too early. } dxe5 11. Nxe5 Nxd5 { His knight takes your d pawn, and the knight you just placed on e5 has nothing guarding it at all. } ) Nbd7 { The knight develops to d7, heading for e5 or c5 later on. Nothing is urgent yet, so ask instead what your own king wants. } 11. O-O { Castled and safe behind your king side pawns. Now bring your pieces out, and aim them at the weak pawn on d6. } ( 11. Be3 { The bishop develops to e3, though the square it really wants is f4. } Re8 12. O-O { Fine as well, but the bishop on e3 never looks at d6 the way f4 would. A similar sort of game either way. } ) ( 11. Bd2 { The bishop retreats to its most passive square on d2, doing nothing toward d6 yet. } Re8 12. O-O { Fine, but the bishop on d2 stays passive there. Castle first and pick f4 for it later. } ) ( 11. Qe2 { Bringing the queen out to e2 is too early here. } Re8 12. O-O c4 { The c pawn hits your bishop with tempo, and his knight gets to jump into c5 next. } ) ( 11. a5 { Pushing the a pawn is not needed yet. Your king still sits in the centre, and that queenside pawn can wait one more move. } Re8 12. O-O { You spent a move on the queenside while your king waited behind. Playable, just less precise than castling first. } ) ( 11. Bf4 { The bishop finds the right square on f4, just at the wrong moment. } c4 12. Bc2 Ne5 { That pawn kicks your bishop away, and his knight lands on e5 with a free tempo. } ) ( 11. Bg5 { The pin from g5 has nothing real behind it yet. } h6 12. Bh4 Re8 { He gains a tempo with the h pawn, and your bishop stays shut out of the d6 diagonal completely. } ) ( 11. e5 { Breaking with the e pawn here is, once again, far too early. } dxe5 12. Nxe5 Nxe5 { That knight on e5 had nothing guarding it at all: a whole piece is simply gone. } ) Re8 { The rook lands on e8, eyeing the e4 pawn. Which of your bishops is still at home, and what does it bite on once it comes out? } 12. Bf4 { From f4 the bishop stares straight down at d6, the one pawn every Benoni structure struggles to defend. } ( 12. Be3 { The bishop on e3 is solid, but it aims at nothing in particular. } Ne5 13. Nxe5 Rxe5 { Fine, you still keep the same pull on the position. The bishop belongs on f4 instead, where it actually aims at something. } ) ( 12. Re1 { Bringing the rook to e1 joins the fight for the e4 pawn. } Ne5 13. Nxe5 Rxe5 { Also fine, and you still keep the pull. Even so, d6 wants a bishop staring straight down at it. } ) ( 12. Bg5 { The pin from g5 is one his knight shakes off easily. } h6 13. Bh4 c4 { The h pawn gains him a tempo, then the c pawn gains him another. Your bishop ends up completely offside for it. } ) ( 12. Qe2 { Guarding the e4 pawn with the queen is another try here. } Ne5 13. Nxe5 Rxe5 { Fine for you, but the queen did nothing useful there. The bishop belongs on f4 instead, where it actually works. } ) ( 12. Bc2 { Stepping the bishop out of the way to c2 is yet another idea. } Nh5 13. Bg5 Bf6 { His bishop chases yours right back. You are simply moving the same pieces twice for nothing. } ) ( 12. Nd2 { Retreating the knight to d2 keeps guard of the e4 pawn. } Ne5 13. Be2 { Passive: that knight only went backwards, and meanwhile his own knight settles comfortably on e5. } ) ( 12. e5 { Breaking with the e pawn here simply gives a pawn away. } Nxe5 13. Nxe5 dxe5 { Knight takes, you take back, and the d pawn takes again. You end the sequence a clean pawn down. } ) Nh5 { The knight jumps to h5, hunting your bishop. Keep the bishop on the same diagonal instead of trading it off. } 13. Bh2 { Tucked into the corner on h2, the bishop still aims at d6 and sits completely out of the knight's reach. } ( 13. Bg5 { The bishop already got chased once here, and this only invites him to chase it again. } Bf6 14. Bd2 Bd4 { His bishop lands on d4, arguably the single best square on the whole board for it. } ) ( 13. Bxd6 { Grabbing the pawn on d6 looks tempting. } Qb6 14. e5 Bxe5 15. Bxe5 Nxe5 { The pawn comes right back to him and his pieces turn active. That bishop was worth more than the single pawn you took. } ) ( 13. Bd2 { Retreating the bishop all the way home to d2 gives up the long diagonal toward d6 completely, instead of tucking it onto h2 and keeping the aim. } h6 14. Be2 { You gave up that whole diagonal for nothing in return. Still fine for you, but the plan behind the bishop is gone. } ) ( 13. Be3 { The bishop sits loose on e3, ready to be harassed by a black knight later on. } Ne5 14. Be2 Nxf3+ 15. Bxf3 { He forced the trades and your bishop had to leave d6 behind. Less pleasant for you than keeping it there. } ) ( 13. Bc1 { Retreating the bishop all the way back to c1 loses two whole moves. } Ne5 14. Be2 { Two moves lost for that bishop, and meanwhile his knights are the ones doing all the active work. } ) ( 13. Bg3 { Offering the trade on g3 is another idea here. } Nxg3 14. fxg3 { Your king side pawns end up doubled and broken. Never let that trade happen. [%csl Rg3][%cal Rh5g3] } ) Ne5 { His best piece, the knight, plants itself on e5. What do you do about it before it settles in for good? } 14. Nxe5 { Trade it now, before that knight settles into e5 for good. Something of his has to recapture, most naturally the bishop on g7. } ( 14. a5 { Pushing the a pawn is also good, since his knight on e5 is not going anywhere just yet. } b5 15. axb6 Rb8 { You capture in passing on b6 and the b column swings open for him. Fine, but bringing the knight in first was the more precise order. } ) ( 14. Be2 { Stepping the bishop back to e2 saves it from the knight on e5 before anything forces the issue, and it simply lets him trade the other knight on f3. } Nxf3+ 15. Bxf3 Nf6 { He trades on f3 himself. Fine for you too, and a similar sort of game either way. } ) ( 14. Re1 { Bringing the rook to e1 joins the fight for the centre. } Nxf3+ 15. Qxf3 Be5 { His bishop takes over the e5 square, and now your own bishop on h2 is completely blocked in. Less comfortable than the main line. } ) ( 14. Bxe5 { Taking with the bishop here is the wrong piece for the job. } Rxe5 15. Nxe5 Bxe5 { You win the exchange, a rook for a knight, but his bishop sitting on e5 holds his whole position together. Only level. } ) ( 14. Nd2 { The knight steps to d2 and blocks your own queen's view of the bishop on d3. } Nxd3 { He takes the bishop on d3 for free, since your own knight just blocked the queen that was guarding it. [%cal Re5d3] } ) Bxe5 { His bishop recaptures on e5 and now eyes your own bishop on h2 along the long diagonal. Do not let him keep that bishop for free. } 15. Bxe5 { His dark-squared bishop is gone. The d pawn recaptures next, and the pawn that lands on e5 blocks his own pieces more than it helps him. } ( 15. f4 { Striking with the f pawn hits the knight, but it also opens the long diagonal for his bishop. } Bd4+ 16. Kh1 Qh4 { His bishop checks you from d4 first, and now his queen arrives on h4 as well. Your king feels every bit of that pressure. } ) ( 15. Qd2 { The queen steps up to d2, guarding the bishop. } Bxh2+ 16. Kxh2 Qh4 { He takes the bishop with check, and his queen reaches h4 with a completely free tempo. } ) ( 15. Qf3 { Bringing the queen out to f3 first is another idea. } Qh4 16. Bxe5 Rxe5 { Level: his queen already sits on h4, so the trade on e5 no longer gains you anything extra. } ) ( 15. a5 { Pushing the a pawn is too soon here. It ignores the bishop on e5 that is eyeing your king, and it lets him seize the attack first with the queen to g5. } Qg5 16. Qf3 Bd7 { His queen and bishop both point at your king now: he is the one doing the attacking here. Level chess at best. } ) ( 15. g4 { The pawn to g4 kicks the knight away. } Bxh2+ 16. Kxh2 Nf4 { That g pawn weakened everything around your king, and now his knight lands on f4 for good. } ) ( 15. Ne2 { The knight steps to e2, guarding both g3 and f4. } Bxh2+ 16. Kxh2 Qh4 { He takes the bishop with check, and the queen comes to h4 with tempo again. Level at best for you. } ) ( 15. Bc4 { The bishop moves to c4 and ignores the threat completely. } Bxh2+ 16. Kxh2 Qh4 { Same story as every other try here: his queen lands on h4 and your king stands right in front of it. } ) dxe5 { He recaptures with the d pawn. His queenside is loose now, on a6 and b7, so which pawn of yours freezes it for good? } 16. a5 { The pawn to a5 locks down his queenside. If he ever pushes b6, the hole it leaves behind can never be covered by a black pawn again. } ( 16. Re1 { Centralising the rook on e1 works well too, instead of freezing his queenside right away, and it can swing to the third row if he ever attacks. } Qg5 17. Re3 { The rook slides across to guard the third row. Good as well, though playing the a pawn first would have fixed his queenside for good. } ) ( 16. Qf3 { Bringing the queen out to f3 is also fine, but it lets him free his position with b6, the very pawn the a pawn push would have frozen. } b6 17. Rfd1 { But now his b pawn is free to move, exactly what the a pawn push would have prevented. } ) ( 16. Kh2 { Stepping the king to h2 is a prudent choice, clearing the long diagonal before anything forces it, though b6 is still allowed. } b6 17. Re1 { Fine, but b6 arrives anyway. Freeze his queenside first next time. } ) ( 16. Bc4 { The bishop finds a good square on c4. } b6 17. Qf3 { Fine too, though his b pawn is now free and his queenside can breathe again. } ) ( 16. b3 { The pawn to b3 is slow. It does nothing about his knight or his queenside, and the knight to f4 arrives next move with real threats. } Nf4 17. Qf3 Qg5 { His knight sits comfortably on f4 and now his queen joins it. That b pawn move gained you nothing at all. } ) ( 16. Nb5 { The knight jumps to b5 with nothing guarding it. } axb5 { The a pawn simply captures it. [%cal Ra6b5] } ) ( 16. f4 { The pawn break on f4 simply drops a pawn. } Nxf4 { The knight grabs the pawn on f4, and the e pawn recaptures if you ever take it back. Level chess either way. } ) ( 16. Qxh5 { Taking the knight on h5 hands the queen straight over. } gxh5 { The g pawn recaptures the queen: a whole queen given up for a single knight. [%cal Rg6h5] } ) Bd7 { His bishop comes out to d7, guarding the queenside pawns. Your own queen wants no part of that side: aim it at his king instead. } 17. Qf3 { The queen swings to f3, hitting the knight on h5 and eyeing f7 at the same time. He has to move that knight right away. } ( 17. Re1 { Bringing the rook to e1 is another useful move here. } Nf4 18. Re3 { This also works well. Playing the queen out first would have forced him to move the knight instead. } ) ( 17. Bc4 { The bishop finds a good square on c4 right away. } Nf4 18. Qf3 { Just as strong: you reach the very same set-up by a different move order. } ) ( 17. Kh2 { Stepping the king to h2 gets it off the long diagonal early. } Qh4 18. Qf3 { His queen beat you to h4 this time. Still fine for you, so bring your own queen out now. } ) ( 17. b3 { The pawn to b3 guards a square that was never even under threat. } Nf4 18. Qf3 { Playable, though that b pawn move was a tempo spent on nothing. } ) ( 17. Ra3 { Lifting the rook to a3 has no target to aim at yet. } Nf4 18. Ne2 b5 { The rook looks odd sitting on a3, and his pieces come alive with moves like this one. Less clear for you than the main line. } ) ( 17. Bxa6 { Grabbing the pawn on a6 looks free, but it is defended. } bxa6 { The b pawn recaptures at once: a whole bishop traded for a single pawn. [%cal Rb7a6] } ) ( 17. Nb5 { The knight jumps forward to b5. } Bxb5 { His bishop simply takes it: nothing was guarding the knight on b5. } ) Nf4 { The knight jumps to f4, hitting both d3 and h3. Move your bishop to a square where it still does real work. } 18. Bc4 { On c4 the bishop is safe from the knight and now stares at f7 and a6 at once. } ( 18. Rfc1 { Swinging the rook to the c column is another idea here. } Qg5 19. Kh2 { Also good: the king steps clear of the checks, and the rook already eyes the c5 pawn from the c column. } ) ( 18. Kh2 { Moving the king to h2 straight away is the other order. } g5 19. Bc4 { Just as sound, though now the bishop still needs to find its square on c4 a move later than in the main line. } ) ( 18. Rfb1 { The rook swings to b1, aiming down the column at b7. } Qg5 19. Kh2 { Fine as well, though the bishop on d3 still sits in the knight's sights until it moves. } ) ( 18. Rfd1 { The rook steps to d1, adding a second guard to the bishop. } Rb8 19. Kh2 { Fine, but now the rook is tied down to guarding the bishop on d3 instead of doing something active. } ) ( 18. Rfe1 { Moving the rook away from f1 toward e1 centralises it, but the bishop on d3 is left just as loose as before. } Qf6 19. Kh2 { His queen finds the f6 square instead, and the bishop on d3 is still hanging loose there. A touch less accurate than the main line. } ) ( 18. Bxa6 { Grabbing the pawn on a6 looks tempting, but the b pawn still guards it, so this only trades a whole bishop for one pawn. } bxa6 { The b pawn simply recaptures. A whole bishop is gone for nothing. } ) ( 18. Qxf4 { Taking the knight on f4 looks free, but it is guarded. } exf4 { The e pawn recaptures the queen: a whole queen given up for a single knight. [%cal Re5f4] } ) Qg5 { His queen joins the knight on the attack, aiming at h3, and the queen on g5 pins your own g pawn to the king. Where does your king feel safest? } 19. Kh2 { Stepping to h2 breaks the pin on your g pawn and dodges every knight check at once. Nothing he owns can reach your king now, and your passed pawn on d5 is worth more than all his checks combined. } ( 19. Qg3 { Offering the queen trade with the move to g3 is another way to answer here, since trading queens shuts down most of his attack. } Qf6 20. Kh2 { He avoids the trade with the queen to f6. Also sound: with the queens now facing each other, his attacking chances shrink. } ) ( 19. Kh1 { Tucking the king into the corner on h1 dodges the same checks, but from there it no longer guards the g3 square the way h2 does. } Reb8 20. Qg3 { This also works, clear of every check. On h2 the king would have guarded g3 as well, which h1 cannot. } ) ( 19. h4 { Pushing the pawn to h4 leaves it completely undefended. } Qxh4 { His queen simply takes the pawn on h4, and now his queen and knight together aim straight at your king. Level chess, his attack balancing the pawn you handed him. } ) ( 19. Rfe1 { Moving the rook off f1 lets his knight jump into h3 next. } Nxh3+ 20. Kh1 { The knight grabs the pawn with check, but your own g pawn now attacks it on h3, so it has to jump out again next move. Roughly level. } ) ( 19. Ra3 { Lifting the rook to a3 does nothing useful on this side of the board. } Bxh3 { His bishop grabs the pawn on h3, and your g pawn is genuinely pinned to the king on g1, so you cannot recapture. Level chess. } ) ( 19. g3 { Pushing the pawn to g3 only weakens h3, since that pawn was its only guard. } Nxh3+ 20. Kg2 { The knight grabs the pawn with check, and stepping the king to g2 walks it straight into the thick of his attack. } ) 1-0


