{ He shoves his pawn to c4, attacking your bishop on d3 and grabbing a tempo. But that pawn now has no support at all — find the move that makes him pay for it. } 9... c4 { His pawn lands on c4, hitting your bishop on d3 with nothing standing behind it. Where does the bishop go to stay useful? [%cal Rc4d3] } 10. Bc2 { The bishop steps to c2. It keeps its aim on h7 and adds a guard to e4, and his pawn on c4 is left with no support of its own. } ( 10. Bxc4 { The bishop grabs the pawn at once on c4, but from there it no longer watches the square e4 — and that pawn is about to become a target. } Re8 11. O-O Nxe4 12. Nxe4 Rxe4 { It is playable, but his rook recaptures on e4 right away, so the pawn you grabbed on c4 was not really free. } ) ( 10. Bb1 { The bishop tucks back to b1, keeping the same diagonal toward h7 without losing the pawn on c4. } Qa5 11. O-O b5 { Black gets his queen to a5 and pushes his pawn to b5, and your rook on a1 sits boxed in behind its own bishop. Little edge is left in this line. } ) ( 10. Be2 { The bishop retreats one square to e2, off the good diagonal and doing far less than it did on d3. } b5 11. O-O b4 { His queenside pawns roll forward with tempo while your bishop on e2 stares at nothing useful. This line is worse for you than the mainline. } ) ( 10. e5 { The pawn pushes straight to e5, breaking open the centre before you have dealt with the bishop on d3 at all. } Nxd5 11. Bxc4 Nxc3 { His knight grabs the pawn on d5 first, and once the dust settles you are down a whole pawn for nothing. [%cal Rf6d5] } ) ( 10. Bf1 { The bishop retreats all the way to f1, giving up two whole tempi just to dodge the pawn on c4. } b5 11. Be2 b4 { You spent two moves tucking the bishop away, and now his queenside pawns roll forward. This line is worse than the mainline. } ) ( 10. O-O { Castling straight away leaves the bishop on d3 hanging to the pawn on c4. } cxd3 { His pawn simply takes the bishop on d3, and you never even got to choose where it retreated. [