{ The Czech Benoni: he locks the centre with e5, and the play is on the king side: h3, g4 and a bishop on h6. } 3... e5 { The centre is locked for good. Your setup starts the same way. What comes first? } 4. Nc3 { The knight guards d5 and makes e4 possible. } ( 4. g3 { Fianchettoes. } d6 5. Nc3 { Also fine: a different setup, and a sound one. } ) ( 4. h3 { The h pawn. } Ne4 5. Qc2 Qa5+ { Playable, though his knight got a free jump and the queen checks. Less. } ) ( 4. e4 { The centre. } Nxe4 5. Qe2 f5 { The pawn comes back with the queen on e2, but he is untangled. Less. } ) ( 4. Qa4 { Checks nothing. } Be7 { Playable, but the queen is early. Less. } ) ( 4. Qc2 { It watches e4 too. } b5 { The queen is early and b5 comes with tempo. Less. } ) ( 4. f3 { Prepares e4. } e4 { The pawn takes f3 from the knight, and his e pawn advances. Less. } ) ( 4. e3 { Timid. The pawn stops short of e4, and now his own pawn claims the square instead. } e4 { The pawn should go to e4 in one step: now his does. Half the edge. } ) ( 4. Nd2 { Comes into play. } e4 { The knight blocks the bishop and his e pawn advances. Half the edge. } ) ( 4. a3 { Sets up b4. } d6 { Slow: b4 is not a threat yet. Half the edge. } ) ( 4. a4 { Shuts down b5. } d6 { The a pawn goes before b5 is a threat. Half the edge. } ) ( 4. Nh3 { Develops. } d6 { The knight is on the rim. Half the edge. } ) ( 4. Qb3 { It watches c4 too. } d6 { The queen is out for no reason. Half the edge. } ) ( 4. Qd3 { Also covers e4. } e4 { The queen is hit at once. Half the edge. } ) ( 4. Bd2 { A developing move. } Ne4 { The bishop does nothing and his knight jumps in. Half the edge. } ) ( 4. Bg5 { A pin. } h6 { The bishop is chased at once. Half the edge. } ) ( 4. Nf3 { Comes into play. } e4 5. Nfd2 { The pawn kicks the knight back. Level. } ) ( 4. dxe6 { Takes on the way past. } fxe6 5. Nc3 d5 { You handed him a sound centre and free pieces. Worse. } ) ( 4. d6 { Pushes. } Qb6 { The pawn is overextended and b2 is hit. Level. } ) ( 4. Bf4 { Develops. } exf4 { The bishop is simply lost. [%cal Re5f4] } ) ( 4. Qd4 { Centralises. } cxd4 { The queen is simply lost. [%cal Rc5d4] } ) d6 { He supports e5. Take the centre. } 5. e4 { The pawn to e4, with the knight behind it. } ( 5. h3 { The h pawn. } Bf5 6. e4 { Also fine: the h pawn comes anyway. } ) ( 5. Qc2 { Guards e4. } Be7 6. e4 { Also fine, though the queen is early. } ) ( 5. g3 { Fianchettoes. } Na6 6. Bg2 { Also fine: a different setup, and a sound one. } ) ( 5. Qd3 { It watches e4 too. } Be7 { Playable, but the queen is early. Less. } ) ( 5. f3 { Prepares e4. } Nh5 { The pawn takes f3 from the knight. Less. } ) ( 5. Qa4+ { Checks. } Bd7 { The check gains nothing: the bishop blocks with tempo. Less. } ) ( 5. e3 { Timid. It leaves e4 for him to grab instead of claiming the square yourself in one move. } e4 { The pawn should go to e4 in one step: now his does. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. Nf3 { A developing move. } e4 6. Nd2 { The pawn kicks the knight back. Most of the edge is gone. } ) ( 5. Bd2 { Comes into play. } e4 { The bishop does nothing and his e pawn advances. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. a4 { Rules out b5. } e4 { The a pawn goes before b5 is a threat. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. Qb3 { Also covers c4. } e4 { The queen is out for no reason. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. Qd2 { Joins the rooks. } e4 { The queen is out for no reason. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. a3 { Gets ready for b4. } e4 { Slow: b4 is not a threat yet. Half the edge. } ) ( 5. Be3 { Develops. } g6 { The bishop is early and a knight on g4 will hit it. Level. } ) ( 5. f4 { Hits e5. } exf4 { The pawn is traded and e4 is loose. Level. } ) ( 5. Nb5 { Jumps at d6. } g6 { The knight has nothing on b5 and a6 sends it home. Level. } ) ( 5. Nh3 { A developing move. } Bxh3 6. gxh3 { The knight is traded and your king side is broken. Level. } ) ( 5. g4 { Grabs space. } Bxg4 { The pawn is simply lost: the knight on f6 guards g4. [%cal Rc8g4] } ) ( 5. Bf4 { Comes into play. } exf4 { The bishop is simply lost. [%cal Re5f4] } ) Be7 { His bishop comes out modestly. Now the pawn move that prepares the storm. } 6. h3 { The h pawn: g4 is prepared, and nothing lands on g4 meanwhile. } ( 6. Bd3 { Develops. } Na6 7. h3 { Also fine: the same setup in another order. } ) ( 6. Nf3 { A developing move. } Na6 7. h3 { Also fine: the same setup in another order. } ) ( 6. a3 { Prepares b4. } Na6 7. Bd3 { Also fine: b4 is a plan of its own. } ) ( 6. Bd2 { Comes into play. } Na6 { Also fine, though the bishop does little on d2. } ) ( 6. Be2 { Develops. } Na6 { Also fine, though d3 is the better square. } ) ( 6. Qc2 { Also covers e4. } Na6 { Also fine, though the queen is early. } ) ( 6. h4 { The h pawn, two steps. } h6 { Also fine: the storm starts early. } ) ( 6. g3 { Fianchettoes. } Na6 { Also fine: a different setup. } ) ( 6. Qf3 { Guards e4. } Na6 { Also fine, though the queen sits where the knight belongs. } ) ( 6. Nge2 { A developing move. } h6 { Playable, but the knight blocks the road of the bishop to d3. Less. } ) ( 6. Qe2 { It watches e4 too. } Na6 { Playable, though the queen blocks the bishop. Less. } ) ( 6. f3 { Also covers e4. } O-O { Playable, but the pawn takes f3 from the knight. Less. } ) ( 6. Qd2 { Links the rooks. } h6 { Playable, though the queen is early. Less. } ) ( 6. Qb3 { Guards c4. } Na6 { Playable, though the queen is out for no reason. Less. } ) ( 6. Qa4+ { Checks. } Bd7 { The check gains nothing: the bishop blocks with tempo. Less. } ) ( 6. Be3 { Comes into play. } Ng4 { His knight jumps to g4 and hits the bishop. Less. [%cal Rf6g4] } ) ( 6. a4 { Stops b5. } Na6 { The a pawn goes before b5 is a threat. Half the edge. } ) ( 6. f4 { Hits e5. } exf4 { The pawn is traded and e4 is loose. Half the edge. } ) ( 6. Nh3 { Develops. } Bxh3 7. gxh3 { The knight is traded and your king side is broken. Level. } ) ( 6. g4 { Grabs space. } Bxg4 { The pawn is simply lost: the knight on f6 guards g4. [%cal Rc8g4] } ) ( 6. Bg5 { Adds a pin. } Nxd5 { The pin fails: whichever pawn takes back, his bishop takes yours on g5. Worse. [%cal Rf6d5] } ) ( 6. Nb5 { Aims at d6. } Nxe4 { The e pawn falls: nothing guards it. Worse. [%cal Rf6e4] } ) ( 6. Bh6 { Sacrifices. } gxh6 { The bishop is simply lost. [%cal Rg7h6] } ) O-O { He castles into the storm. Develop the knight where it belongs. } 7. Nf3 { The knight to f3, not e2: e2 is the road of the bishop to d3. } ( 7. Bd3 { A developing move. } h6 8. Nf3 { Also fine: the same setup in another order. } ) ( 7. Be3 { Comes into play. } Na6 8. Nf3 { Also fine: the bishop eyes c5. } ) ( 7. Bd2 { Develops. } h6 8. Nf3 { Also fine, though the bishop does little on d2. } ) ( 7. g3 { Fianchettoes. } Na6 { Also fine: a different setup. } ) ( 7. a3 { Sets up b4. } h6 { Also fine: b4 is a plan of its own. } ) ( 7. Be2 { A developing move. } h6 { Also fine, though d3 is the better square. } ) ( 7. Qc2 { Guards e4. } Na6 { Also fine, though the queen is early. } ) ( 7. Nge2 { Comes into play. } a6 { Playable, but the knight blocks the road of the bishop to d3. Less. } ) ( 7. h4 { The h pawn again. } h6 { Playable: the storm starts before the pieces are out. Less. } ) ( 7. Qf3 { It watches e4 too. } h6 { Playable, though the queen sits where the knight belongs. Less. } ) ( 7. Qa4 { Eyes a7. } Ne8 { Playable, but the queen is out for no reason. Less. } ) ( 7. Qd2 { Connects. } h6 { Playable, though the queen is early. Less. } ) ( 7. Qb3 { It watches c4 too. } Ne8 { Playable, though the queen is out for no reason. Less. } ) ( 7. a4 { Shuts down b5. } Na6 { The a pawn goes before b5 is a threat. Less. } ) ( 7. g4 { The storm. } h6 { The pawn goes before the pieces are out. Less. } ) ( 7. Qe2 { Also covers e4. } h6 { The queen blocks the bishop. Less. } ) ( 7. f4 { Hits e5. } exf4 { The pawn is traded and e4 is loose. Half the edge. } ) ( 7. f3 { Guards e4. } Nh5 { The pawn takes f3 from the knight and his knight heads for f4. Level. } ) ( 7. Bg5 { Pins. } Nxd5 { The pin fails: whichever pawn takes back, his bishop takes yo