e knight goes to the rim. Half the edge. } ) ( 14. Rdf1 { Prepares f4. } Ng8 { The rook belongs on g1. Half the edge. } ) ( 14. Kc2 { Walks. } Ng8 { The king walks by hand. Half the edge. } ) ( 14. Nb1 { Retreats. } Ng8 { The knight goes home for nothing. Half the edge. } ) ( 14. Kd2 { Walks. } Ng8 { The king walks into the centre. Half the edge. } ) ( 14. Rd2 { Guards. } Ng8 { The rook takes the retreat square of the bishop. Most of the edge is gone. } ) ( 14. Bxg7+ { Trades. } Kxg7 { The bishop pair is gone and the dark squares are his again. Level. } ) ( 14. Ng5 { Jumps. } Ng8 { The knight has nothing on g5 and the bishop on h6 is hit. Level. } ) ( 14. a5 { Fixes b6. } Qxa5 { The pawn is simply lost. [%cal Rd8a5] } ) ( 14. g5 { Pushes. } Nfh5 { The pawn goes too early: his knight sits on h5 for good. Level. } ) ( 14. Bg5 { A pin. } Nxd5 { The pin fails: the knight takes on d5 and hits your bishop. Worse. [%cal Rf6d5] } ) ( 14. h4 { The h pawn. } Nxg4 { The pawn is simply lost: the h pawn no longer guards g4. [%cal Rf6g4] } ) ( 14. Nb5 { Jumps at d6. } axb5 { The knight is simply lost. [%cal Ra6b5] } ) Bd7 { He develops. His knight can step to g8 and hit your bishop: retreat it in advance. } 15. Bd2 { The bishop steps back before the knight can hit it, and every piece you own is already aimed at his king on h8 — the pawns on g4 and h3 are just the start. } ( 15. Ne1 { Finds a new route. } Ng8 16. Bd2 { Playable, though the knight is passive. Less. } ) ( 15. Be3 { Falls back. } Qa5 { Playable, though the bishop can be hit by a knight on g4 later. Less. } ) ( 15. Kb1 { Sidesteps. } Qa5 { Playable, but nothing is gained. Less. } ) ( 15. Nd2 { Takes the long way. } Ng8 { Playable, but the knight goes backwards. Less. } ) ( 15. Bc2 { Heads home. } b5 { The bishop retreats for nothing and b5 comes. Half the edge. } ) ( 15. Rg3 { Lifts. } Ng8 { The rook is early on g3. Half the edge. } ) ( 15. b3 { Guards c4. } Ng8 { The b pawn does nothing for the attack. Half the edge.